{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01022 - MONDO_1566",
  "verbalization": "Phylloquinone is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hypercalcemia Disease through a series of biochemical pathways involving calcium metabolism. The evidence suggests that Phylloquinone is associated with Nephrocalcinosis, a condition characterized by the deposition of calcium salts in the kidneys, which in turn leads to the formation of calcium compounds such as Calcium Acetate, Calcium Glucoheptonate, and Calcium Glycerophosphate. These calcium salts are known to contribute to hypercalcemia by increasing calcium levels in the blood. The consistent association across three distinct paths indicates a robust mechanism by which Phylloquinone may influence calcium homeostasis, potentially through the promotion of calcium deposition and subsequent elevation of serum calcium concentrations. The overlapping pathways highlight a shared role in calcium metabolism, suggesting that Phylloquinone may act as a modulator of calcium excretion or retention, thereby contributing to the development of hypercalcemia. This integrated evidence underscores the importance of calcium metabolism in the pathophysiology of Hypercalcemia Disease and supports the hypothesis that Phylloquinone may play a role in its development.",
